  "claims": [
    {
      "text": "Flips of a fair coin are independent: whatever came before, the chance of tails on the next flip is 1/2.",
      "source": "OpenStax, Introductory Statistics 2e, §3.2 Independent and Mutually Exclusive Events (openstax.org)",
      "checkedBy": null,
      "beats": [
        "l2",
        "l4",
        "l6",
        "l8"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Over many flips the share of tails gets close to 1/2 (the law of large numbers), because the streak becomes a smaller part of the total, not because later flips make up for it.",
      "source": "OpenStax, Introductory Statistics 2e, §3.1 Terminology (law of large numbers)",
      "checkedBy": null,
      "beats": [
        "l7"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "simplifications": [
    "Assumes a fair coin and a fair toss. Real tossed coins land on the side they started on slightly more often than half (about 51% in a 350,757-flip study, Bartoš et al. 2023). That comes from the toss, not from memory of earlier flips.",
    "The histogram is a simulation of a fair coin, not a recording of real flips."
  ],
